From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 23:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] macos: pause and resume a running VM (krun_pause,
 krun_resume)

Add krun_pause(ctx) / krun_resume(ctx) so a host can freeze a VM in memory
(the steamac launcher's "Suspend") and continue it later in the same process.

Pause: VmPause (one per started context, registered by krun_start_enter
like krun_display_resize's DisplayControl) sets a flag, kicks every vCPU
out of hv_vcpu_run with hv_vcpus_exit and waits (at most 1 s) until each
vCPU thread is parked on a condvar before re-entering the guest, or is
outside the guest anyway (waiting for CPU_ON, or in a userspace-GIC WFE
wait). Parked threads use no CPU; guest memory and all device/GPU state
stay in place. The virtio-snd CoreAudio backend stops its units while
SndControl::paused is set (and does not start them for a PREPARE/START
that arrives meanwhile): the render callback stops consuming the ring, the
guest's hw_ptr stops after at most the host FIFO, and playback carries on
from the same sample on resume, without underruns.

Resume: the paused interval (from the moment the last vCPU left the guest)
is added to every vCPU's virtual counter offset (hv_vcpu_set_vtimer_offset,
applied by each vCPU on its own thread before it enters the guest again),
so the guest's CNTVCT, CLOCK_MONOTONIC, timers and watchdogs see no gap,
as with QEMU's default for KVM and HVF on arm64 (kvm-adjvtime). Without it
(measured, 94 s pause) SteamOS's sched_ext scheduler is killed by its
watchdog ("scx_lavd disabled (runnable task stall)", with a stack trace).
CLOCK_REALTIME stays behind by the paused time until the guest's NTP
client steps it. STEAMAC_PAUSE_CLOCK=running keeps the counter running
(the guest sees a jump) for comparison.

The WFx trap's timeout now compares CNTV_CVAL with the virtual counter
(mach_absolute_time() minus the vCPU's offset) instead of the host counter.
